<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-potential-temperature" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Poisson's equation and potential temperature</title>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-dry-adiabatic-lapse-rate" text="type-global" />
        gives the rate at which a perfectly dry air parcel cools with increasing height as it rises adiabatically
        (or the rate at which it warms with decreasing height as it sinks adiabatically).
        But it does not give the actual temperature of such an air parcel at any point during its ascent
        (or descent).
    </p>

    <p>
        To find the temperature <m>T</m> a perfectly dry air parcel would have after moving adiabatically
        from its initial pressure level <m>p_0</m> with initial temperature <m>T_0</m>
        to pressure level <m>p</m>,
        set <m>\delta q = 0</m> in <xref ref="eqn-first-law-alt" text="type-global" />,
        rearrange <xref ref="eqn-ideal-gas-law-specific-volume" text="type-global" /> to replace <m>\alpha = \dfrac{R_dT}{p}</m>,
        separate,
        and integrate.
        This results in <term>Poisson's equation</term>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-poissons-equation" number="yes">
            \frac{T}{T_0}
            =
            \left(
                \frac{p}{p_0}
            \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that <xref ref="petty" text="custom">Petty (2008)</xref> and other texts substitute
        <m>\kappa = \dfrac{R_d}{c_p}</m>
        into <xref ref="eqn-poissons-equation" text="type-global"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="eqn-poissons-equation" text="type-global"/>
        is used to define <term>potential temperature</term> <m>\theta [K]</m>
        by replacing <m>T_0</m> with <m>\theta</m>
        and rearranging to solve for <m>\theta</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-potential-temperature" number="yes">
            \theta
            =
            T \left(
                \frac{p_0}{p}
            \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        We interpret <m>\theta</m> as the temperature a perfectly dry air parcel would have
        after moving adiabatically to pressure level <m>p_0</m> from its current pressure level <m>p</m> where it has temperature <m>T</m>.
        Thus,
        for any given value of <m>p_0</m>,
        a perfectly dry air parcel has a unique, conserved value of <m>\theta</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Usually,
        we set <m>p_0 = 1000 \, \text{hPa}</m> as a mathematically simple approximation to Earth's standard mean sea level pressure,
        although any convenient reference value can be used.
    </p>

</subsection>